  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The dashboard has two panels, and the alert rule is enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has the time field `timestamp`. The response field is `response`, with `response.keyword` for exact matching.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-d8cf2f72 Web logs' has two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of request count by `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - \"Responses by code\" (renamed in step 4): a bar chart of request count by `response.keyword`, top 20 values.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-d8cf2f72 5xx errors' is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, with time field `timestamp`. It runs every 1m and fires when the count is above 10 over a 5-minute window. Server errors are matched with the wildcard `response.keyword: \"5??\"`, which covers 500\u2013599. The rule was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n4. **Read-back:** The panel title is 'Responses by code'. The rule reads back as `enabled: true`, status `ok`.\n\nOne choice to note: `response` is a string field, so a numeric range wouldn't work on it. The `5??` wildcard is how I covered 500\u2013599.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
